<ResearchGuideLayout
  title="How to Find a Certificate of Analysis by Lot Number"
  description="Find the Certificate of Analysis for your exact lot, check the original report, and know what to do if the lot or report number does not match."
  eyebrow="LOT LOOKUP GUIDE"
  heading="How to find a COA by lot number"
  introduction="Start with the lot or batch code on the label. Use it to find the original Certificate of Analysis (COA) and check that the report covers the material you received."
  datePublished="2026-11-14"
  dateModified="2026-11-14"
>
  <form action="/coa" method="get" class="rgv-guide-lookup" aria-labelledby="lot-lookup-label">
    <label id="lot-lookup-label" for="lot-lookup">Search the RGVPRIME certificate library</label>
    <div>
      <input id="lot-lookup" name="q" type="search" placeholder="Enter the lot number, SKU, or product name" required />
      <button type="submit">Find certificates</button>
    </div>
  </form>

  <h2>Match the label to the report</h2>
  <ol class="rgv-guide-steps">
    <li><div><strong>Copy the lot code from the label.</strong><span>Look for LOT, Batch, or a similar field on the container or its packaging. Copy letters, numbers, and punctuation. Save the product name and SKU separately.</span></div></li>
    <li><div><strong>Search the library and open the original PDF.</strong><span>If the lot appears in an earlier record, open that version. For a search by SKU or product name, compare the label with each candidate report before choosing one.</span></div></li>
    <li><div><strong>Check the lot and sample description in the PDF.</strong><span>Compare the lot, product identity, and any format or strength shown. Record the report number and issue date so someone else can retrieve the same document.</span></div></li>
    <li><div><strong>Check the issuer if you need to verify the source.</strong><span>Use the named laboratory’s own lookup portal if available. If the lab uses a separate verification code, use the code shown on the report. If there is no public lookup, ask the issuer how to confirm the report number.</span></div></li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Keep the SKU, lot, and report number separate</h2>
  <dl class="rgv-guide-identifiers">
    <div><dt>SKU</dt><dd>Identifies the catalog configuration. The same SKU can appear on more than one lot.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Lot or batch number</dt><dd>Identifies the batch. Use the code on the label when matching your material to a certificate.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Report number</dt><dd>Identifies the document in the issuer’s records. A laboratory may also assign its own sample ID.</dd></div>
  </dl>

  <h2>What to do when something does not match</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Same product, different lot.</strong> Treat the PDF as documentation for the lot printed on that report. Search the exact code on your label and check earlier reports.</li>
    <li><strong>Only a lab sample ID appears.</strong> Ask the supplier for a record that connects the sample ID to the lot on your label.</li>
    <li><strong>Two PDFs show the same report number.</strong> Compare issue dates and revision information. If the difference is unclear, confirm the current version with the issuer before filing it.</li>
    <li><strong>No matching report appears.</strong> <a href="/contact">Contact RGVPRIME support</a> with the product name, SKU, and exact lot code. Include a photo of the label if requested through the support channel.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="rgv-guide-callout">
    <strong>Example with fictional identifiers</strong>
    <p>If your label says <code>DEMO-042</code> and the PDF says <code>DEMO-041</code>, the product name alone cannot resolve the mismatch. Keep the two records separate and request the document for <code>DEMO-042</code>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Save a record your team can audit</h2>
  <p>Keep the original PDF with the product or catalog identifier, lot code, issuer, report number, issue date, and date you retrieved it. For help interpreting individual fields, see <a href="/research-guides/how-to-read-a-certificate-of-analysis">how to read a Certificate of Analysis</a>.</p>
  <p>For an example of supplier documentation search organized by identifier, see <a href="https://www.thermofisher.com/us/en/home/support/documents-certificates.html">Thermo Fisher Scientific’s document and certificate lookup</a>.</p>
</ResearchGuideLayout>
